  <h1>Is Hitch rideshare available in California?</h1>
  <div class="meta">Updated August 2026</div>

  <p class="lede"><strong>Short answer: no.</strong> Hitch — the city-to-city rideshare that's made a real business out of Texas routes like Dallas–Houston and Austin–San Antonio — doesn't operate in California. If you've used it in Texas and want the same door-to-door intercity trip on the West Coast, here's what exists and how it differs.</p>

  <h2>What Hitch is</h2>
  <p>Hitch proved something important: Americans will happily share a car between cities if you make it bookable. On its Texas routes it offers scheduled, door-to-door rides between metros a few hours apart — the trips that are too short to fly sensibly and too long for a normal rideshare fare. It's a real, operating company, and if your trip is inside Texas, it's a genuine option.</p>

  <h2>Where it stops</h2>
  <p>Hitch's network is Texas. Search a California route — San Francisco to Los Angeles, LA to San Diego, LA to Las Vegas — and there's nothing to book. Intercity networks like this are built route by route, because they only work when enough riders and drivers want the same city pair on the same day.</p>

  <h2>The California equivalent — and the model difference</h2>
  <p>On the West Coast, <strong>Seat Sherpa</strong> covers California and Nevada's main routes. The rider experience is similar — book a seat in advance for a city-to-city trip — but the economics are deliberately different:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Hitch (Texas)</th><th>Seat Sherpa (CA &amp; NV)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Who drives</td><td>Recruited, compensated drivers</td><td class="win">Everyday drivers already making the trip</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pricing model</td><td>Commercial fares set by the platform</td><td class="win">Cost-sharing — capped at the IRS mileage rate split across seats</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical intercity seat</td><td>Varies by route and demand</td><td class="win">SF–LA $40–55 · LA–San Diego $15–28 · LA–Vegas $35–50</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Verification</td><td>Driver vetting</td><td class="win">Phone + government ID + driver's licence, two-way ratings</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Payment</td><td>In-app</td><td class="win">Paid in-app, held safely and released when a pickup code confirms the trip started</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Texas routes</td><td>California &amp; Nevada routes</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p>The practical difference: because Seat Sherpa drivers can't profit — seat prices are hard-capped at the trip's actual cost — prices track gas money rather than market demand, and there's no surge. The trade-off is that departures follow real drivers' plans rather than a fixed timetable, which is also what keeps the seats cheap.</p>

