  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Does Hitch operate in California?</h3><p>No. Hitch runs city-to-city rideshare on Texas routes like Dallas–Houston and Austin–San Antonio. It has no California network. For California and Nevada intercity trips, Seat Sherpa fills the same role with a cost-sharing carpool model.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>What is the California version of Hitch?</h3><p>The closest California equivalent is Seat Sherpa — a city-to-city marketplace covering routes like San Francisco–Los Angeles, LA–San Diego and LA–Las Vegas. The model differs: Seat Sherpa is cost-sharing (seat prices capped at the real cost of the drive), while Hitch is a commercial intercity service.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is Hitch a carpool or a rideshare?</h3><p>Hitch is closer to commercial intercity ridesharing — drivers are recruited and compensated for trips. A cost-sharing carpool like Seat Sherpa is different: drivers are everyday people already making the drive, and seat prices are capped so they recover costs rather than profit.</p></div>

